<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Turkey</h1><p class="st-meta">1923 CE – present · capital Ankara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The nation state founded by Mustafa Kemal Ataturk on the ruins of the Ottoman Empire, spanning Anatolia and eastern Thrace.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/government-of-the-grand-national-assembly">Government of the Grand National Assembly</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">War of Independence</a>, abolished the Ottoman sultanate and secured recognition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Lausanne</a> in July 1923. On 29 October 1923 the assembly proclaimed the republic, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> as capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a> as president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Ataturk</a>'s reforms abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> (1924), replaced Islamic law with European-based codes, introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet (1928), gave women the vote (1934) and made secularism a constitutional principle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismet-inonu">Ismet Inonu</a> continued one-party rule until multi-party elections brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adnan-menderes">Adnan Menderes</a> to power in 1950. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1952. The army intervened in 1960, 1971 and 1980, and Kurdish insurgency began in 1984.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> became an EU candidate in 1999. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recep-tayyip-erdogan">Recep Tayyip Erdogan</a>, prime minister from 2003, became president in 2014, survived a coup attempt in 2016 and introduced a presidential system in 2018. In 2022 the country adopted Turkiye as its international name.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-turkey.jpg" alt="Republic of Turkey" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David Benbennick (original author) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Turkey.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1923 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ankara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mustafa Kemal Atatürk, İsmet İnönü, Recep Tayyip Erdoğan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Turkey, Türkiye</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1974&amp;focus=republic-of-turkey" aria-label="Republic of Turkey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5489 749 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Turkey lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5809 1005L5709 970M5809 1005L5911 1127M5809 1005L5809 1005M5809 1005L6072 1024"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5911 1127h0M5809 1005h0M6072 1024h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5809 1005h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5809 1005h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ankara. <a href="/atlas?year=1974&amp;focus=republic-of-turkey">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
